1. SUMMARY

   1.1 help.php - unvalidated path in a PATH_INFO-driven file server (CWE-22).

   help.php built a filesystem path from $_SERVER['PATH_INFO'] and passed it
   straight to readfile(). It validated the UI and locale components and never
   validated the page tail, so on any front-end that hands PHP a path still
   containing ".." segments the endpoint read files outside the help tree - the
   obvious target being lib/settings.php, which defines DB_HOST/DB_NAME/DB_USER/
   DB_PASSWORD in the clear and therefore reaches every record in the system. The
   endpoint also served help content to callers with no session at all. Measured
   on modern front-ends (Apache 2.4.68, nginx 1.31.6), the traversal does NOT
   reach PHP: both servers normalise dot segments before PATH_INFO is built. This
   is a real code defect with deployment-dependent reachability, not a
   demonstrated anonymous file read - see 3.1 for what was and was not measured.
   Fixed in <RELEASE-VERSION>.

   1.2 relay.php - authenticated SQL injection cluster (CWE-89).

   The relay dispatches a method named by the request, with arguments taken from
   the request, and its only gate was "is someone logged in". Four of the methods
   it can reach concatenated request data into SQL. A session holder could read
   arbitrary rows out of the database: reading the full `user` table, including
   the password digests, was reproduced live (3.2). The same
   concatenation idiom appeared in roughly 170 further sites across ~60 files
   reachable by the same mechanism, so the fix is the four sites plus a
   tree-wide sweep plus a deny-by-default allowlist at the relay. Fixed in
   <RELEASE-VERSION>. The allowlist ships LOG-ONLY; enforcement is a documented
   operator step (4.1).

   1.3 vcalendar.php - pass-the-hash and cross-provider calendar access
       (CWE-287 / CWE-836, CWE-639).

   The endpoint accepted a password digest as the credential itself:
   ?user=<name>&hash=<md5 of the password> was compared directly against the
   stored user.userpassword, so anyone holding the digest was authenticated
   without knowing the password. Live where register_argc_argv is Off - which is
   what php.ini-production ships, i.e. the usual production install - and masked
   where it is On (PHP's compiled default), because the script's own argv CLI
   guard then halts every query-string request with a silent empty 200 (3.3).
   The same endpoint let any authenticated caller read any provider's calendar by
   naming that provider in ?physician=, with no authorization check. Fixed in
   <RELEASE-VERSION>; the GET user/hash path is disabled by default behind the
   vcals_legacy_hash compatibility switch (4.2).


2. AFFECTED / NOT AFFECTED, AND WHERE THE FIX IS

   2.1 Finding 1 - the layouts this is reachable on. "Affected" below means the
       pre-fix code was reached with a traversing path; it is measured, not
       assumed.

   layout                                        pre-fix result        affected
   Apache 2.4.68 + mod_php 8.3.35                  ..  -> 400            no
     (/help.php/gwt/en_US/../../../../etc/passwd)  (normalised path escapes
                                                    the root)
     same, ..%2f form                              -> 404               no
     same, %2e%2e%2f form                          -> 404               no
     (re-tested with AllowEncodedSlashes On and
      AcceptPathInfo On: still 404)
   nginx 1.31.6 + php-fpm 8.3.35                   all forms -> 400      no
   Apache < 2.4.49, and any proxy/FastCGI layout
     that preserves dot segments in PATH_INFO      not measurable here   YES
                                                   (code-level)

   In EVERY layout above the pre-fix endpoint served help content anonymously:
   GET /help.php/gwt/en_US/main answered 200 with 5818 bytes both with and
   without a session. That is the exposure the auth half of the fix removed.

   The fix: lib/help-path.php (new - validates the page token, resolves with
   realpath() on both sides, requires the basename to equal
   <page>.<locale>.html, refuses dot segments and a missing/unreadable target)
   and help.php (a Login.LoggedIn gate before any filesystem access, plus a
   single generic 404 that does not disclose whether a path exists). Commit
   3949591f. The sibling PATH_INFO consumer chtml.php had the same
   "validate some parameters, not the tail" shape and is fixed with an
   end-anchored token (ee5ee372, 4f2eee6f); the full sink audit is
   tests/security/evidence/path-sink-audit.md.

   2.2 Finding 2 - every layout that serves relay.php, on both web servers, and
       only for a caller holding a valid session.

   * Apache 2.4.68 + mod_php: the injected calls returned the leaked rows (live).
   * nginx 1.31.6 + php-fpm: the same four methods produced the same leaked rows
     (spot check, same session, same payloads).
   * unauthenticated: refused before the method runs. The same call with no
     cookie and with a made-up SessionID both answer INVALID_SESSION - measured
     on the injected payload itself
     (tests/security/evidence/relay-sqli-prefix.txt, SITE 1).
     Only the org.freemedsoftware.public.* namespace bypasses the login guard,
     and none of the four sites is in it.
   * NOT an entry point: POST /relay.php/json with a JSON body. It fatals on
     Relay_Json::deserialize_parameters(), which is called at
     Relay_Json.class.php:87 and :90 and defined nowhere in the tree. The working
     vector is the method name in PATH_INFO plus param0..paramN form fields.

   The fix: the four injection sites (c7e17354 ModuleSearch::picklist,
   c71a74c8 EncounterNotesTemplate::getTemplates, 04248424
   UserInterface::GetUsers, 606bbd11 Remitt statement rendering), the
   SqlIdent identifier validator and the base-class identifier invariants
   (a5060a09) with the tree-wide sweep in batches on top, and the relay
   allowlist (1ae0e45f) whose default is log-only (4.1).

   2.3 Finding 3 - conditional on one PHP ini setting, and both conditions were
       measured.

   * register_argc_argv = Off (php.ini-production's value; the usual Debian/
     Ubuntu production install and this harness's container): the GET user/hash
     path AUTHENTICATES on the digest alone. The three outcomes are
     distinguishable - see 3.3 for the exact status codes, byte counts and
     bodies.
   * register_argc_argv = On (PHP's compiled default; the stock php:8.3-apache
     image ships no php.ini, so the default applies there): every request
     carrying a query string is halted by the script's own CLI guard
     (if ($_SERVER['argc'])) with a masked empty 200 - no status, no body, no
     log line. The endpoint neither authenticates nor refuses anything, so on
     that layout neither the vulnerability nor its fix is observable.
   * The fix no longer depends on this setting at all -
     commit f0d53619 replaces the argv test with if (PHP_SAPI === 'cli'), which
     no ini file can move. Re-measured under both settings with the same results
     (6 probes, byte-identical; vcalendar-auth.txt §6.2).
   * The Basic-auth branch - the branch clients are told to migrate to - was DEAD
     on PHP 8.3 before this work: vcalendar.php:62 called a global prepare() that
     is declared nowhere in the tree, so any credentialed request without the
     query credential died with "Uncaught Error: Call to undefined function
     prepare()". Repaired in the same change.

   The fix: vcalendar.php (574ac616 reject the GET-hash path, 62af8f2b scope the
   provider to the authenticated caller, f0d53619 the SAPI guard) plus
   Physician.class.php (7a60d3ab the count() TypeError, 12622abe the !empty($d)
   refinement).


3. FINDINGS IN DETAIL

   Every measurement quoted below is copied from a file under
   tests/security/evidence/ that is committed with this branch. Where a claim is
