   3.1 FINDING 1 - help.php arbitrary file read (CWE-22), CVSS 3.1

   MECHANISM. help.php:46 (pre-fix) read a path assembled at help.php:40-44 from
   $_SERVER['PATH_INFO']. The regexes at help.php:35-38 were applied to $parts[1]
   (UI) and $parts[2] (locale) only; the page tail reached the concatenation
   unvalidated, and the ".html" suffix at help.php:48-50 was attached only if
   that variant happened to exist, so a non-matching request was readfile()d raw
   at help.php:57. There was no session or ACL check anywhere in the file:
   help.php:24-57 includes lib/freemed.php and nothing else. So the defect is
   two independent things - an unvalidated path sink, and content served to
   anonymous callers - and the fix closes both.

   MEASURED EVIDENCE - the traversal does NOT reproduce on modern front-ends
   (help-traversal-prefix.txt, section B; captured with curl --path-as-is, which
   matters: without it curl collapses the dot segments client-side and the row
   measures nothing):

     Apache :38081, no cookie, pre-fix
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/../../../../etc/passwd              HTTP 400
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/%2e%2e%2f%2e%2e%2fetc/passwd        HTTP 404
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/..%2f..%2fetc/passwd                HTTP 404
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/....//....//etc/passwd              HTTP 200
             body: "Help index ..../..../etc/passwd not present."
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/%252e%252e/%252e%252e/etc/passwd    HTTP 200
             body: "Help index %2e%2e/%2e%2e/etc/passwd not present."
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/..%5C..%5Cetc/passwd                HTTP 200
             body: "Help index ..\..\etc/passwd not present."

     nginx :38082 is the same except that the single-encoded rows also reach PHP
     with an EMPTY page token (fastcgi_split_path_info no longer matched), which
     is the same PHP 8.3 "Undefined array key 1" path noted in the sink audit:
       /help.php/gwt/en_US/%2e%2e%2f%2e%2e%2fetc/passwd        HTTP 200
             body: "Help index  not present."

     NO PAYLOAD RETURNED FILE CONTENTS on either server. The shapes that do reach
     PHP arrive as literal text that fails file_exists() and prints the
     not-present line. The hardened repro, run against the same pre-fix tree:
       5 probe(s): 0 exploited, 4 refused/filtered before PHP (400/404),
                   0 auth required (401/403), 1 answered otherwise
       >>> RESULT: NO EXPLOIT OBSERVED (measured negative, exit 0)
     The counter "0 auth required" is the point: pre-fix there is nothing to be
     401'd.

   MEASURED EVIDENCE - the anonymity defect, which is what the harness could
   demonstrate end to end:

     pre-fix  Apache :38081, NO cookie   GET /help.php/gwt/en_US/main
              STATUS: 200   bytes: 5818
     pre-fix  Apache :38081, WITH a valid session, same request
              STATUS: 200   bytes: 5818   (identical body)
     pre-fix  nginx :38082, with and without a session: 200 / 5818 bytes both
     post-fix anon   GET /help.php/gwt/en_US/main
              HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized   body: "Authentication required."
     post-fix authed GET /help.php/gwt/en_US/main
              HTTP 200, 5818 bytes, unchanged help HTML

   SEVERITY. The vector a CVE record should carry for the code defect is the
   reporter's, and it is right for a layout where the traversal is reachable:

       AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N = 7.5   (High)

   Reasoning in one line: network-reachable, no session, no user interaction,
   read-only confidentiality impact at the level of the database credentials in
   lib/settings.php, and scope unchanged because the file read stays inside the
   web user's own authority. The severity is DEPLOYMENT-DEPENDENT, and that
   qualifier belongs in the record: on the two modern front-ends measured here
   the reachability is zero, and what remains demonstrated on them is the
   anonymous serve of help HTML, i.e.

       AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N = 5.3   (Medium)

   and only if a site treats its own help text as non-public. On a site where it
   does not, the measured impact on those layouts is nil and the fix is
   defence-in-depth plus an independently justified authentication requirement.
   The execution record labels it that way (ruling R4) and this advisory does not
   claim a live anonymous file read on any modern stack.

   WHAT THE FIX DOES. Authentication before any filesystem access (anonymous
   requests get 401), and resolution that cannot leave the help directory: every
   path component is validated, realpath() is applied to both the help directory
   and the candidate, the candidate's basename must equal
   <page>.<locale>.html, and the file must exist and be readable. Nothing about
   the fix depends on the front-end normalising anything, so it holds on the
   layouts where the pre-fix code would have traversed. The ".html" suffix is now
   deterministic, so a request for /main and /main.en_US both resolve to
   main.en_US.html - the contract the GWT client's help button uses.

