   SEVERITY. The GET-hash path, as a code-level defect:

       AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N = 5.9   (Medium)

   Reasoning in one line: no session is needed (PR:N) - the digest itself is the
   credential - but the attack requires the attacker to already hold that stored
   digest, which is the AC:H condition. If a scorer instead treats the digest as
   available through this same release's other defects (3.2 reads it out of the
   database for a session holder), the chained view scores higher and that is a
   chain argument, not this endpoint's own vector. The configuration qualifier is
   part of the record: MASKED where register_argc_argv is On, live where it is
   Off.

   The cross-provider branch, scored on the endpoint's purpose - a provider's
   calendar holds patient-linked appointments:

       AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N = 6.5   (Medium)

   with the measured floor stated honestly:

       AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N = 4.3   (Medium)

   We are not claiming calendar contents were disclosed. The measured fact is
   that the provider parameter was honoured for any authenticated caller (the
   pre-fix body for the non-physician caller is byte-identical to the admin's) and
   that the fix refuses it with 403; the content half could not be demonstrated
   because the harness cannot populate that calendar (3.4). Certificates-of-impact
   language belongs in the CVE record only with that qualifier attached.

   WHAT THE FIX DOES. freemed_get_auth() and its call site are gone: the endpoint
   is Basic-auth over TLS, with a proper 401 challenge (repaired - the challenge
   died inside the endpoint on PHP 8 before this change) and the credential
   comparison moved out of SQL into PHP, fetching the row by username and
   comparing with hash_equals(). The pre-authentication syslog line is removed.
   The GET path exists only behind the off-by-default vcals_legacy_hash option
   (4.2). The provider is the authenticated caller's own unless ?physician=
   names another provider AND the caller holds the schedule/view grant, which is
   evaluated fail-closed; ?physician plus type/m/d/y are integer-validated, and
   an impossible date (m=13) is refused with 400 instead of being normalised by
   mktime().

   3.4 LIMITATIONS - WHAT THIS ADVISORY DOES NOT CLAIM

     (a) NO CLIENT SMOKE PASS WAS POSSIBLE. This checkout contains no compiled
         GWT client (find ui/gwt -name '*.js' returns only vendored viewerjs and
         mugshot resources), so no browser session has ever exercised the relay
         call set. That is exactly why the new allowlist ships LOG-ONLY, and why
         enforcement is documented as an operator step with a miss-collection
         pass rather than as a released behaviour (4.1).

     (b) The module-smoke functional gate cannot see a valid-but-wrong query that
         returns 0 rows. The committed baseline has 136 entries: 6 OK, 22 EXC,
         108 SKIP, and the SKIPs come from the reduced seed - an empty table makes
         a correct query and a wrong-but-valid query indistinguishable. "No
         deltas" is therefore not proof that a batch's SQL is equivalent, only
         that the observed outcomes did not move.

     (c) THE HARNESS IS A THROWAWAY STACK WITH A REDUCED SEED. Its tables are
         pinned by tests/security/evidence/seed-fingerprint.md (with
         seed-verify.sql) because the seed genuinely drifted once during this
         work. Patient tables are empty: `patient`, `procrec` and `vitals` have 0
         rows, so no PHI exists in the harness at all and no PHI was disclosed by
         anything measured here.

     (d) FINDING 1 WAS NOT DEMONSTRATED END TO END ON A MODERN FRONT-END, and the
         reporter's proof of concept was confirmed in isolation - by replicating
         the logic - not end to end. Any published reproduction steps must say so,
         which is why 5.2 corrects them.

     (e) FINDING 3's PHI IS NOT DEMONSTRATED. The reproduced vCalendar body is
         the envelope only - BEGIN:VCALENDAR / VERSION:1.0 /
         PRODID:<provider's own name> / TZ:-07 / END:VCALENDAR - with no event
         entries, because `scheduler` is empty and cannot be populated here: an
         insert fails with ERROR 1452 (a foreign key from patient_emr to the
         pinned-empty `patient` table). Two pre-existing PHP 8.3 defects also sit
         between this and a client-parseable calendar (core/vCalendar.class.php's
         uninitialised $buffer printing a warning into the payload, and
         core/Physician.class.php:83 handing an array to strpos()), both recorded
         in doc/SECURITY_FOLLOWUP rather than fixed here. So: authentication
         bypass demonstrated live; calendar contents reproducible on a populated
         install.

     (f) FINDING 2's PRE-FIX ROWS COME FROM A RECONSTRUCTED TREE. The fixes had
         already landed, so the pre-fix rows were measured against a throwaway
         extraction of the pinned pre-fix commit served from the harness docroot,
         with the vulnerable concatenation re-verified by grep in the served file
         before each run. Two of the four sites' script-based verdicts are the
         script's own thresholds rather than the leak's absence (3.2), and the
         committed rows for them are hand-transcribed from the live responses.

     (g) VERIFICATION WAS PRIMARILY ON APACHE + mod_php 8.3.35; nginx 1.31.6 +
         php-fpm was used for spot checks and for the deployment rules
         (four servers for the latter: two harness copies and two mounting the
         shipped configuration files themselves).

     (h) EVIDENCE HYGIENE. tests/security/evidence/sweep-notes.md:369 prints the
         harness verification account's literal MD5 digest - the pass-the-hash
         payload 3.3 removed - which contradicts this work's own no-digest rule
         for evidence. It is scheduled for a one-line redaction in a later fix
         pass. The honest limit: the value is already in this branch's git
         history, so redacting the working tree does not remove it; it is a
         throwaway harness credential for a synthetic account; and neither this
         advisory nor any CVE request may quote it.


4. WHAT THE RELEASE CHANGES FOR OPERATORS

   Read this section before upgrading. Items marked MANDATORY are steps only the
   operator can take; the release cannot do them for you.

   4.1 THE RELAY IS DENY-BY-DEFAULT, AND IT SHIPS LOG-ONLY

   relay.php now checks the requested method against an allowlist
   (data/config/relay-allowlist.php, matcher in
   lib/org/freemedsoftware/core/Relay_Allowlist.class.php) before dispatching,
   after the login guard, for every method including the public.* namespace. A
   method that is not named in the list is a MISS.

   SHIPPED BEHAVIOUR: 'enforce' => false. A miss is logged at LOG_WARNING and the
   call STILL EXECUTES. You get the safety of the enumeration and a log of what
   your site actually calls; you do not get enforcement until you ask for it.

   MANDATORY OPERATOR STEP (if you want the control enforced): run the procedure
   in doc/RELAY_ALLOWLIST section 3 - deploy log-only, confirm by hand that a
   deliberate miss logs, use the system normally on your real compiled client
   until the log is quiet, add every method named there to 'patterns', then set
   'enforce' => true. Proven live in both states: with enforcement on, an unlisted
   method returns the body INVALID_CALL (HTTP 200 - the relay's own response
   convention) and does not run; with it off, the same call runs and logs
   "Relay: method '<name>' is not in the relay allowlist (remote=<ip>, LOG-ONLY,
   call still executed)".

   Also changed, and worth knowing even before you enforce: the allowlist now
   covers the two re-dispatch axes. A relay method that re-dispatches a method
   name supplied by its caller (UserInterface.Multicall) gates the inner call
   through the same decision point, and the ModuleInterface wrappers -
   add/del/GetRecord/GetRecords/mod/picklist/RenderHtmlView/to_text and the print
   wrappers' RenderToPDF - gate the concrete org.freemedsoftware.module.<Class>.
   <literal> string they are about to dispatch, because a per-method list cannot
   constrain a class the caller names. Measured exposure on that axis: 866
   (class, literal) pairs exist over the 133 module class files and 803 of them
   are NOT in the shipped list, so enforcing will refuse a wrapper call naming a
   module class the list does not name - by design, and that is what the
   log-only period exists to discover. Separately, 'never_allow' refuses INNER
   calls in the org.freemedsoftware.core. namespace in BOTH stages (the pre-2.8
   Multicall guard's behaviour, reinstated); outer calls in that namespace are
   logged and not refused. A config edit is not instant and the OPcache lag is
   PER WORKER - probe more than once before believing an answer. A missing or
   unparseable data file logs and falls back to log-only rather than refusing
   everything.

   4.2 vCALENDAR: THE GET CREDENTIAL PAIR IS DEPRECATED AND OFF BY DEFAULT

   http://host/vcalendar.php?user=<name>&hash=<md5 of the password> no longer
   authenticates: such a request gets 401 with the Basic challenge. The
   compatibility switch, DEFAULT OFF, is a config row:
       INSERT INTO config (c_option, c_value, c_title, c_section, c_type)
       VALUES ('vcals_legacy_hash', 'off', 'vCalendar legacy GET hash auth',
               'Scheduler', 'YesNo');
   and every use of it is logged at LOG_NOTICE as "DEPRECATED hash authentication
   used (vcals_legacy_hash=on), claimed username = <user>, remote = <ip>" -
   whether or not the credential is accepted. Search for that string to find the
   clients that still need migrating; turn the row off when the log is quiet.
   Nothing in a request can enable it.

   MANDATORY OPERATOR STEP: migrate calendar clients to HTTP Basic over TLS
   before upgrading (they get 401 otherwise), or set the row above for the
   migration window.

   MANDATORY OPERATOR STEP - and this one can remove a capability that works
   today: ?physician=<other provider> now requires the schedule/view access
   control grant for any caller that is not the administrator and is not that
   provider. On a schema where that grant has never been created the check fails
   closed and the request is refused with 403. A front desk or practice-manager
   account that used to fetch providers' calendars will lose that ability until
   you create the schedule/view ACO and the AROs for the roles that need it. See
   doc/VCALENDAR_AUTHENTICATION section 4 for the full prerequisite. Related, and
   worth a look while you are there: this endpoint loads the ACL on demand because
   a SESSION_DISABLE request skips the block in lib/freemed.php that builds it,
   and if the vendored phpGACL cache directory under /tmp is not writable by the
   web user the check is refused rather than permitted.
   Other interface changes: ?type, ?m, ?d, ?y are integer-validated and an
   impossible date is refused with 400 instead of being normalised; the endpoint's
   own CLI guard now tests PHP_SAPI rather than $_SERVER['argc'].

   4.3 WEB-SERVER RULES: FOUR TREES DENIED, PATH_INFO SCOPED

   Shipped: doc/freemed.apache.conf (installed by doc/Makefile as
   /etc/apache2/conf.d/freemed.conf) and docker/nginx.conf, with the .htaccess
   files as a second layer that nginx does not read at all.

   MEASURED AT HEAD, BEFORE THE RULES - this is why the rules are not a nicety:
     Apache /lib/API.php                     200, 0 bytes (EXECUTED)
     Apache /lib/settings.php                200, 0 bytes (EXECUTED)
     Apache /scripts/*.sh                    200, 3107/8563 bytes (SOURCE)
     nginx  the same trees plus
            /data/schema/mysql/patient.sql  200 as SOURCE,
            tests/security/... 200 as SOURCE,
            /lib%2FAPI.php EXECUTED
     both   /data/store/no-image.png         200, 60521 bytes
            (a malformed `<Files *> deny from *` in that directory's .htaccess
             took the subtree OUT of the parent data/ denial)
     both   data/documents/unfiled/*.djvu    200, 60521 bytes
   AFTER: every one of those is 403 on both servers, including /lib%2FAPI.php and
   /help.php/../scripts/... . lib/settings.php was reachable and executed on both
   servers (the source was not disclosed by mod_php/php-fpm), which is an
   unauthenticated entrypoint to the file that holds the database credentials -
   now 403 everywhere.
   NOTE for the honest reading: this is not an independent vulnerability report
   about lib/settings.php - it is the same release's hardening, and any layout
   where the PHP handler does not cover the path (a static server, a proxy serving
   .php as text) discloses the credentials to whoever asks. These rules are the
   belt; fix your handler configuration if you still get the source.

   PATH_INFO is scoped: AcceptPathInfo stays On for the install root because five
   entrypoints route on the trailing path (help.php, chtml.php, relay.php,
   relay-gwt.php, controller.php), and any OTHER script addressed with a trailing
   path is refused with 403. MEASURED BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, three shapes, both
   servers, before -> after: /install.php/<path> 200 -> 403, /index.php/<path>
   302 -> 403, /vcalendar.php/<path> 401 -> 403, /services/RemittCallback.php/
   <path> 200 -> 403. None of them reads PATH_INFO and the plain URLs are
   unchanged (/services/RemittCallback.php?wsdl is still 200, verified).
   The allowlist is ANCHORED to the request root - an earlier revision of the rule
   was an unanchored substring test, so /install.php/x/help.php/ ran the installer
   instead of being refused (measured 200 before, 403 after). A site serving the
   tree under a different prefix must add that prefix to the nginx map entry the
   same way it must add its own location block; the Apache rule matches in
   per-directory context and is prefix-independent (measured in docroot,
   /freemed, and a second Alias).

   4.4 register_argc_argv = Off

   docker/php-custom.ini now sets it Off, and a site with its own php.ini should
   add the same line. Why it matters beyond vcalendar.php: PHP's compiled default
   for this setting is On, so without the line a request carrying a query string
   looks like a command line to any script that guards on $_SERVER['argc'] /
   $_SERVER['argv'], and the request halts as an empty 200 - which is what masked
   finding 3 on the stock image. Provenance, stated exactly: this was measured on
   the verification image (built FROM php:8.3-apache, no active php.ini), NOT on
   the Bitnami-layout image this project's docker-compose.yml ships, which was not
   available to inspect and normally loads a php.ini that already sets it Off. The
   explicit line is still the right thing to ship - wherever the file is read it
   wins - but do not read it as "the trap is live on your container". The CLI SAPI
   ignores the setting, so a command-line script must test PHP_SAPI, not this.

   4.5 THE .htaccess CORRECTIONS

   Four files carried <Files *> deny from * - and * is not the `all` keyword a
   Deny takes, so it matched no client and the rule allowed everything it was
   meant to deny (that is how /scripts/*.sh served source, and how data/store/'s
   file cancelled its parent's denial). Five more carried 2.2-only order/deny
   syntax, which on an Apache 2.4 without mod_access_compat is a configuration
   error during URI mapping - the tree answers 500 rather than the 403 the rule
   means (measured by config test with the module set minus mod_access_compat).
   All nine are rewritten with a module-guarded dual-syntax block. One deliberate
   behaviour change: data/documents/unfiled/ denied its *.djvu exception, so
   unfiled .djvu uploads are no longer fetchable by URL (200 -> 403; the document
   viewer fetches page images through the relay, not the filesystem path).
   lib/org/freemedsoftware/acl/.htaccess is deliberately left 2.2-only: it is
   vendored phpGACL, not this tree's file.

   4.6 DOCUMENTS ADDED, AND THE RELEASE-NOTE TEXT

   doc/DEPLOYMENT_HARDENING   what the shipped web-server configuration refuses,
                              the PATH_INFO scope, the ini, and the lib/
                              settings.php measurement and verdict
   doc/RELAY_ALLOWLIST        the allowlist, the two re-dispatch axes, the
                              never-allow rule, and the enforcement procedure
   doc/VCALENDAR_AUTHENTICATION  the deprecation, the migration steps, the
                              compatibility switch and the schedule/view
                              prerequisite
   doc/SECURITY_FOLLOWUP      the backlog of defects found and deliberately not
                              fixed here - read it before reporting one of them
   This repository has no CHANGELOG or release-notes file; the release-note
   paragraphs for the relay and for vCalendar are sections 4 and 5 of those two
   documents and should be published with them.

   4.7 THE MANIFEST OF MANDATORY STEPS

     1. Install the new web-server configuration (package install does it; a
        self-managed vhost must copy the four <Directory> blocks, or the nginx
        deny location, into its own config) and the corrected .htaccess files.
     2. Add register_argc_argv = Off to your php.ini if you run your own.
     3. Migrate vCalendar clients from ?user=&hash= to Basic over TLS, or set
        vcals_legacy_hash for the migration window.
     4. Grant the schedule/view ACO and AROs to any non-admin role that reads
        providers' calendars other than its own.
     5. After a normal use period, complete the relay allowlist's
        miss-collection pass and set 'enforce' => true if you want it enforced.


