       (i)  TRAVERSAL DEPTH IS OFF BY TWO. Against the documented install root
            /usr/share/freemed (Makefile:27 INSTDIR; the docker mount agrees):
              /gwt/en_US/../../../../lib/settings.php   -> /usr/share/freemed/lib/settings.php
              /gwt/en_US/../../../lib/settings.php      -> .../ui/lib/settings.php (does not exist)
              seven or more `..` reach an absolute path (/etc/passwd), and an
              eighth/ninth clamp at the root.
            In a checkout at a deeper path the count is larger again (9 `..` are
            needed for /etc/passwd from this checkout), so a reproducer must
            either run from the install root or count its own depth.

       (ii) THE TRAVERSAL DOES NOT REPRODUCE ON MODERN FRONT-ENDS. Measured
            2026-09-28: Apache 2.4.68 answers 400 for `..` and `%2e%2e` and 404
            for `..%2f`; nginx 1.31.6 answers 400 for all forms; re-tested with
            AllowEncodedSlashes On and AcceptPathInfo On, still 404. Both servers
            normalise dot segments before PHP builds PATH_INFO, so the path never
            reaches the vulnerable concatenation there. The code is
            unconditionally unsafe and IS exploitable on a front-end that
            preserves dot segments (Apache < 2.4.49, some proxy/FastCGI
            layouts), but "confirmed dynamically" describes a PoC confirmed in
            isolation - replicating the logic - not end to end on a running
            deployment. The advisory reports it that way (3.1).

       (iii) THE PHASE-2 ENTRY POINT IS DIFFERENT FROM THE ONE REPORTED.
            POST /relay.php/json with a JSON {"method":…,"params":[…]} body
            cannot execute: it fatals on Relay_Json::deserialize_parameters(),
            called at Relay_Json.class.php:87 and :90 and defined nowhere in the
            tree. The working vector, proven live, puts the method name in
            PATH_INFO and the arguments in param0..paramN form fields (3.2). The
            reproduction in any published advisory must use the working vector;
            the JSON-body transport is separately recorded in
            doc/SECURITY_FOLLOWUP section 4.3 as a functional defect.


