                                  session (Relay.class.php:45-51, pre-fix;
                                  lib/loader.php:47-70).
     Impact:                      An authenticated user could read arbitrary rows
                                  out of the database. Reproduced live: a UNION
                                  SELECT through UserInterface.GetUsers returned
                                  the entire user table including the password
                                  digests (an unsalted MD5 of each password);
                                  the same technique leaked names through
                                  ModuleSearch.picklist, template fields through
                                  EncounterNotesTemplate.getTemplates, and a
                                  username:digest string through
                                  Remitt.RenderStatementXML. Because the relay's
                                  reachable surface is every public method of
                                  every registered class, the same class of
                                  defect existed in write paths too (fixed in the
                                  same release, not proven live).
     Affected component:          relay.php and the relay-reachable API/module
                                  surface, remediated by parameterizing every
                                  application-owned concatenation (the four
                                  sites plus a tree-wide sweep using ->quote(),
                                  integer casts and a validated-identifier
                                  helper) and by a deny-by-default method
                                  allowlist at the relay
                                  (data/config/relay-allowlist.php).
     Attack vector:               AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N = 6.5 for the
                                  proven read impact. If the record is scoped to
                                  the reachable surface rather than the four
                                  sites, the same vector with I:H/A:H is
                                  AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H = 8.8
                                  (integrity/availability assumed from the
                                  write-path instances fixed in the release, not
                                  proven live). Prerequisite, measured: a valid
                                  session - unauthenticated and bogus-session
                                  calls answer INVALID_SESSION before the method
                                  runs, so PR:L and not PR:N.
     Discovery credit:            kta1kri
     References:                  <RELEASE-ANNOUNCEMENT-URL>; fix commits
                                  c7e17354, c71a74c8, 04248424, 606bbd11,
                                  1ae0e45f; evidence
                                  tests/security/evidence/relay-sqli-prefix.txt;
                                  advisory <ADVISORY-URL>

   7.A.3  REQUEST 3 - vCalendar pass-the-hash and cross-provider access

     Request type:                Report Vulnerability/Request CVE ID
     Vendor:                      freemed
     Product:                     FreeMED (electronic medical record system);
                                  distribution package name "freemed"
     Version(s) affected:         all releases prior to <RELEASE-VERSION>; the
                                  defects were reproduced against the 0.9.x line
     Version fixed:               <RELEASE-VERSION> (<RELEASE-DATE>)
     Vulnerability type:          Use of a password hash instead of the password
                                  for authentication, i.e. pass-the-hash
                                  (CWE-836; improper authentication, CWE-287);
                                  authorization bypass through a user-controlled
                                  key (CWE-639) for the provider parameter. The
                                  underlying unsalted-MD5 storage (CWE-916) is
                                  NOT fixed in this release and is out of scope.
     Root cause:                  vcalendar.php's freemed_get_auth() accepted
                                  ?user=<name>&hash=<md5 of the password> and
                                  compared the request-supplied value directly
                                  against the stored user.userpassword
                                  (pre-fix vcalendar.php:66-71), so the stored
                                  digest was itself a usable credential. The
                                  same endpoint selected the calendar from
                                  $_REQUEST['physician'] with no comparison
                                  against the authenticated caller
                                  (pre-fix vcalendar.php:85-88).
     Impact:                      Anyone holding a user's stored MD5 password
                                  digest - from a database read, a backup, a log,
                                  or the SQL injection fixed in the same release
                                  - could authenticate to the vCalendar endpoint
                                  without knowing the password, and could then
                                  name ANY provider and receive that provider's
                                  calendar. MEASURED: the digest alone
                                  authenticated (three distinguishable outcomes
                                  for a correct digest, an impossible digest and
                                  no credential), the provider parameter was
                                  honoured for a caller with no relationship to
                                  that provider (a non-physician caller's
                                  response for a named provider was
                                  byte-identical to the administrator's), and
                                  the calendar CONTENTS were not demonstrated -
                                  the reproduced body is the empty calendar
                                  envelope with the provider's name. Two
                                  pre-existing PHP 8 defects also made the
                                  calendar unreachable (an undefined prepare()
                                  and a count() TypeError), both repaired in the
                                  same release.
                                  CONFIGURATION QUALIFIER, part of the finding:
                                  the GET-hash path authenticates where
                                  register_argc_argv = Off (what
                                  php.ini-production ships, i.e. the usual
                                  production install) and is masked where it is
                                  On (PHP's compiled default), because the
                                  endpoint's own argv CLI guard then halts every
                                  query-string request with a silent empty 200.
     Affected component:          vcalendar.php, remediated by removing the
                                  GET-hash authentication path (Basic over TLS
                                  only, disabled-by-default vcals_legacy_hash
                                  compatibility switch), moving the credential
                                  comparison out of SQL and into PHP with
                                  hash_equals(), and scoping the provider to the
                                  authenticated caller with a fail-closed
                                  schedule/view access-control check.
     Attack vector:               Pass-the-hash:
                                  AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N = 5.9
                                  (AC:H: the attacker must already hold the
                                  stored digest; PR:N: no session is needed).
                                  Cross-provider access:
                                  AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N = 6.5,
                                  with the measured floor
                                  AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N = 4.3
                                  because calendar contents were not
                                  demonstrated.
                                  Recommended record score: 6.5 (the highest of
                                  the defensible vectors), with both vectors and
                                  the configuration qualifier in the
                                  description.
     Discovery credit:            kta1kri
     References:                  <RELEASE-ANNOUNCEMENT-URL>; fix commits
                                  574ac616, 62af8f2b, f0d53619, 7a60d3ab;
                                  evidence
                                  tests/security/evidence/vcalendar-auth.txt,
                                  tests/security/evidence/vcalendar-provider-scope.txt;
                                  advisory <ADVISORY-URL>

