   7.B.3  TO THE REPORTER (kta1kri)

     Subject: FreeMED - your report, what we fixed, and when we will publish

     Thank you for the report - all three findings were real and two of them
     were reproduced live against a running stack. We are crediting you as
     kta1kri in the advisory, the release notes and the CVE records; tell us if
     you would rather be credited differently or not at all.

     Being straight with you about the three places our measurements differ
     from the proof of concept, because the advisory's reproduction steps should
     be exact:

       1. The traversal depth is two segments more than the report states. From
          the documented install root, 4 x ".." reaches lib/settings.php and 7+
          reaches an absolute path.
       2. The traversal does not reproduce on modern front-ends: Apache 2.4.68
          returns 400 for ".."/"%2e%2e" and 404 for "..%2f"; nginx 1.31.6
          returns 400 for every form; re-tested with AllowEncodedSlashes On and
          AcceptPathInfo On, still 404. Both normalise dot segments before PHP
          builds PATH_INFO. The code is unsafe as you wrote, and exploitable on
          a front-end that preserves dot segments (Apache < 2.4.49, some
          proxy/FastCGI layouts) - but your "dynamically confirmed" step was
          confirmed in isolation, by replicating the logic, rather than end to
          end. The advisory says exactly that.
       3. The relay entry point in the report does not execute: POST
          /relay.php/json with a JSON body fatals on
          Relay_Json::deserialize_parameters(), which is defined nowhere in the
          tree. Your injection is real and we reproduced it with the method name
          in PATH_INFO and the arguments as param0..paramN form fields, e.g.
          UserInterface.GetUsers with param1="x' UNION SELECT username,
          userpassword FROM user-- -", which returns the user table including
          the digests.

     We are not claiming a live calendar-content disclosure for the vCalendar
     item: the digest authenticates (measured, on a stack where
     register_argc_argv is Off) and the provider parameter was honoured for any
     authenticated caller, but the calendar body we could reproduce was the
     empty envelope, so the advisory says "authentication bypass demonstrated
     live; contents reproducible on a populated install".

     Timeline: CVE IDs requested <CVE-REQUEST-DATE> (we will forward them to
     you), release <RELEASE-VERSION> on <RELEASE-DATE>, advisory published
     <PUBLISH-DATE>. If you plan a write-up, tell us the date and we will
     coordinate - we would rather publish the same day than in a sequence.
     We have asked downstream packagers to hold until <EMBARGO-UNTIL>.

     <MAINTAINER-NAME> <MAINTAINER-EMAIL>

